    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so it's just a matter of the TTL settings and the various ISPs holding onto the old info until the timer runs out? it feels like the data just... disappears or gets stuck in transit sometimes. is there any technical reason why it's so inconsistent?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, the inconsistent behavior is largely due to how DNS resolution protocols handle caching and query normalization across different providers. When a resolver receives a query, it may return different cached TTL values depending on the upstream server's configuration, which can make the perceived delay feel unpredictable even if the underlying timeout is standard. This is a fundamental design feature of DNS to improve performance, ensuring that local networks don't constantly re-query the root or TLD servers for every request. There is no magical \"unstick\" button, but consulting your hosting provider's about page might reveal specific server-side limitations affecting your current TTL rates.",
      "source": "target",
